#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179367 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9696b3f4-42c8-5796-be3a-6c59c9600c95" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Work Done by a Force"}] :question/id #uuid "622bd0ff-d08a-5504-8b38-8bc388bfcbf5" :question/math-academy-id "e-12525" :question/problem "A particle $P$ of mass $3\\,\\rm{kg}$ moves along the $x$-axis with an acceleration of magnitude $(1 + 2x^2)\\,\\rm{m/s}^2$ acting in the direction $OP.$ Given that $x$ is measured in meters, calculate the work done by the force in moving the particle from $x=0$ to $x=2.$" :question/worked-solution "The work done by a force $F=F(x)$ in moving a particle from $x=x_1$ to $x=x_2$ is given by\n\n$$\nW = \\int_{x_1}^{x_2} F(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nWhen the force and distance are measured in Newtons and meters, respectively, the work done by the force is measured in Joules ($\\rm{J}$).\n\nUsing Newton's second law of motion, the force required to accelerate a particle of mass $3\\,\\rm{kg}$ at a rate of $(1 + 2x^2)\\,\\rm{m/s}^2$ is\n\n$$\nF = ma = 3 \\cdot (1 + 2x^2) = (3 + 6x^2) \\,\\rm{N}.\n$$\n\nNow, substituting the known values into our integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(3+6{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[3x+2{x}^{3}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =3(2)+2({2}^{3})-(3(0)+2({0}^{3})) \\\\  & =6+16-0 \\\\  & =22\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179368 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ec390427-7510-57ea-aedf-a930284f93b5" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Kinetic Energy of a Particle"}] :question/id #uuid "4e428ee4-0017-5e83-887c-26e723b4af08" :question/math-academy-id "e-12526" :question/problem "A particle $P$ of mass $2\\,\\rm{kg}$ moves along the $x$-axis from rest at the origin with an acceleration of magnitude $(4t+1)\\,\\rm{m/s}^2,$ where the acceleration acts in the direction of increasing $x,$ and $t \\geq 0$ is the time in seconds. Calculate the change in kinetic energy of the particle between the times $t = 1\\,\\rm{s}$ and $t = 2\\,\\rm{s}.$" :question/worked-solution "The change in kinetic energy of a particle with mass $m,$ initial velocity $v_0,$ and final velocity $v_1$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta \\, \\text{KE} = \\dfrac{1}{2}mv_{1}^{2} - \\dfrac{1}{2}mv_{0}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWhen the mass and velocity are measured in kilograms and meters per second, respectively, the particle's kinetic energy is measured in Joules ($\\rm{J}$).\n\nFirst, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ v(t) & =\\int (4t+1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ v(t) & =2{t}^{2}+t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(0) = 0.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n0 = 2(0^2) + 0 + C\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\nC = 0.\n$$\n\nHence, the velocity of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t) = 2t^2 + t.$ In particular, its inital velocity at time $t = 1\\,\\rm{s}$ and final velocity at time $t = 2\\,\\rm{s}$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{0} & =v(1)=2({1}^{2})+1=3\\,m/s, \\\\ {v}_{1} & =v(2)=2({2}^{2})+2=10\\,m/s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the change in kinetic energy of the particle between these times is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ\\,\\text{KE} & =\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot {10}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot {3}^{2} \\\\  & =100-9 \\\\  & =91\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179369 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "94904fa2-bf02-5486-950c-979a4d12998e" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Work-Energy Principle To Find the Work Done by a Force"}] :question/id #uuid "39eab788-1a8d-51af-b535-594cacc8ec2e" :question/math-academy-id "e-12527" :question/problem "A particle $P$ of mass $1\\,\\rm{kg}$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of a resultant force. The displacement of the particle is given by $(t^2-2t+3)\\,\\rm{m},$ where $t \\geq 0$ is the time in seconds. Calculate the work done by the force between the times $t=1\\,\\rm{s}$ and $t=3\\,\\rm{s}.$" :question/worked-solution "The work-energy principle states that the work done by a resultant force on a particle equals the change in the particle's kinetic energy. That is,\n\n$$\nW\n= \n\\Delta\\, \\text{KE}\n$$\n\nwhere the change in kinetic energy of a particle is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta\\, \\text{KE} = \\dfrac12 m v_1^2 - \\dfrac12 m v_0^2\n$$\n\nand $m$ is the particle's mass, $v_0$ is its initial velocity, and $v_1$ is its final velocity.\n\nThe displacement of the particle is given by $x(t)=t^2-2t+3,$ where $t$ is time in seconds.\nWe find the velocity $v(t)$ of the particle by differentiating the displacement with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-2t+3) \\\\  & =2t-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the particle's initial velocity, at $t=1$, is\n\n$$\nv_0 = v(1) =  2(1) -2 = 0\\,\\rm{m/s},\n$$\n\nand its final velocity, at $t=3$, is\n\n$$\nv_1 = v(3) = 2(3) -2= 4\\,\\rm{m/s}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the work-energy principle, the work done by the force between these times is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =Δ\\,\\text{KE} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1\\cdot (4)^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1\\cdot {0}^{2} \\\\  & =8-0 \\\\  & =8\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179370 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "793154ca-29f0-5552-97cd-57acbc915966" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Work-Energy Principle to Find a Particle's Velocity"}] :question/id #uuid "8f79d7e9-377f-5012-a762-279839fc806a" :question/math-academy-id "e-12528" :question/problem "A particle $P$ of mass $2\\,\\rm{kg}$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of a force of magnitude $(x+3)\\,\\rm{N}$ acting in the direction of increasing $x.$ Given that $x$ is measured in meters, and the particle has velocity $v=2\\,\\rm{m/s}$ at $x=2,$ calculate the velocity of the particle at $x=4.$" :question/worked-solution "The work-energy principle states that the work done by a resultant force on a particle equals the change in the particle's kinetic energy. That is,\n\n$$\nW\n= \n\\Delta\\, \\text{KE}\n$$\n\nwhere the change in kinetic energy of a particle is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta\\, \\text{KE} = \\dfrac12 m v_1^2 - \\dfrac12 m v_0^2\n$$\n\nand $m$ is the particle's mass, $v_0$ is its initial velocity, and $v_1$ is its final velocity.\n\nFirst, we calculate the work done by the force in moving the particle from $x=2$ to $x=4{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & ={\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}F(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{4}(x+3)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+3x]}_{2}^{4} \\\\  & =(8+12)-(2+6) \\\\  & =12\\,J\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe particle has velocity $v=2\\,\\rm{m/s}$ at $x=2.$ So, we can find the velocity $v_1$ at $x=4$ using the work-energy principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2} \\\\ 12 & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot {v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot {2}^{2} \\\\ 12 & ={v}_{1}^{2}-4 \\\\ {v}_{1}^{2} & =16 \\\\ {v}_{1} & =\\pm 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can disregard the negative solution since the velocity and acceleration of the particle are positive in the regions under consideration. \n\nTherefore, the velocity of the particle at $x=4$ is $4\\,\\rm{m/s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180030 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9696b3f4-42c8-5796-be3a-6c59c9600c95" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Work Done by a Force"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "999c4d55-0cbb-5dcc-a421-563bcdceed78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178181} {:answer/id #uuid "0d2dfd7e-0b27-55db-809f-ca51f98cc0cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178182} {:answer/id #uuid "43f22f78-4a1e-5622-9b3f-47a164b39748" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178183} {:answer/id #uuid "5407afe9-51b6-5c08-a6e4-c5884596940d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178184} {:answer/id #uuid "787f62d6-efa2-5ec5-ba5c-fec241cb9282" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178185}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0d2dfd7e-0b27-55db-809f-ca51f98cc0cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178182} :answer-field/id #uuid "00d967dc-6c93-55d9-bc06-7bec8bf5cf28" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178180}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8cf39bb1-9c63-5747-9e30-28aba079140a" :question/math-academy-id "q-163596" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of a force of magnitude $2{x}^{2}\\,\\text{N}$ acting in the direction of increasing $x.$ Given that $x$ is measured in meters, calculate the work done by the force in moving the particle from $x=0$ to $x=1.$" :question/worked-solution "The work done by a force $F=F(x)$ in moving a particle from $x={x}_{1}$ to $x={x}_{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\nW={\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}F(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWhen the force and distance are measured in Newtons and meters, respectively, the work done by the force is measured in Joules ($J$).\n\nSubstituting the given values into our integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3}{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}(1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180034 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "94904fa2-bf02-5486-950c-979a4d12998e" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Work-Energy Principle To Find the Work Done by a Force"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "83958cd5-98b4-5471-a193-169760a3ee04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178205} {:answer/id #uuid "cf031512-cf61-58c0-8fd1-fe11a824b3ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178206} {:answer/id #uuid "583c566d-6b5d-5150-8b94-779112bc705e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178207} {:answer/id #uuid "6314d0ec-acd7-5f07-994c-2182f8c3880e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178208} {:answer/id #uuid "78672ea8-12be-5075-8a76-f2e2057f7676" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178209}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "83958cd5-98b4-5471-a193-169760a3ee04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178205} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc01404c-6fba-52a3-9293-2058b5de8e64" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178204}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c92a85dc-fea5-582a-a4d0-c749606f94b3" :question/math-academy-id "q-163601" :question/problem "A particle $P$ of mass $2\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of a resultant force. The particle's velocity is $2t\\,m/s,$ where $t\\ge 0$ is the time in seconds. Calculate the work done by the force between the times $t=1\\,s$ and $t=2\\,s.$" :question/worked-solution "The work-energy principle states that the work done by a resultant force on a particle equals the change in the particle's kinetic energy. That is,\n\n$$\nW=Δ\\,\\text{KE}\n$$\n\nwhere the change in kinetic energy of a particle is given by\n\n$$\nΔ\\,\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2}\n$$\n\nand $m$ is the particle's mass, ${v}_{0}$ is its initial velocity, and ${v}_{1}$ is its final velocity.\n\nThe particle's velocity is given by $v(t)=2t,$ where $t$ is time in seconds. So, the particle's initial velocity, at $t=1$, is\n\n$$\n{v}_{0}=v(1)=2(1)=2\\,m/s,\n$$\n\nand final velocity, at $t=2$, is\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=v(2)=2(2)=4\\,m/s.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the work-energy principle, the work done by the force between these times is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =Δ\\,\\text{KE} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot (4)^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot (2)^{2} \\\\  & =16-4 \\\\  & =12\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319800 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "94904fa2-bf02-5486-950c-979a4d12998e" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Work-Energy Principle To Find the Work Done by a Force"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34113ccc-051e-5d0b-bd95-a5057d7b8b3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319802} {:answer/id #uuid "f3d8af87-2f59-5e5b-a6b8-28af47d706e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319803} {:answer/id #uuid "9b6e4cb5-ad07-5494-9efd-5599005987b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319804} {:answer/id #uuid "ec2aa5ef-c10c-5b26-a07c-c09856ccc90c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "17\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319805} {:answer/id #uuid "06100131-f71c-52c1-88e1-67af380ee926" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319806}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "06100131-f71c-52c1-88e1-67af380ee926" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319806} :answer-field/id #uuid "4e60c184-afc4-5553-a0d0-f418d78f5027" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319801}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8305d2ef-8fb9-5f80-b2fe-3b5b2380f4bf" :question/math-academy-id "q-163671" :question/problem "A particle $P$ of mass $1\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of a resultant force. The particle's velocity is $(t+1)\\,m/s,$ where $t\\ge 0$ is the time in seconds. Calculate the work done by the force between the times $t=2\\,s$ and $t=4\\,s.$" :question/worked-solution "The work-energy principle states that the work done by a resultant force on a particle equals the change in the particle's kinetic energy. That is,\n\n$$\nW=Δ\\,\\text{KE}\n$$\n\nwhere the change in kinetic energy of a particle is given by\n\n$$\nΔ\\,\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2}\n$$\n\nand $m$ is the particle's mass, ${v}_{0}$ is its initial velocity, and ${v}_{1}$ is its final velocity.\n\nThe particle's velocity is given by $v(t)=t+1,$ where $t$ is time in seconds. So, the particle's initial velocity, at $t=2$, is\n\n$$\n{v}_{0}=v(2)=2+1=3\\,m/s,\n$$\n\nand final velocity, at $t=4$, is\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=v(4)=4+1=5\\,m/s.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the work-energy principle, the work done by the force between these times is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =Δ\\,\\text{KE} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1\\cdot (5)^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1\\cdot (3)^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{2}-\\frac{9}{2} \\\\  & =8\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319807 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "94904fa2-bf02-5486-950c-979a4d12998e" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Work-Energy Principle To Find the Work Done by a Force"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c904c404-150b-5876-98af-45d26b5ab085" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "253\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319809} {:answer/id #uuid "816aa354-a06a-51d3-93f2-de665c5bef82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "275\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319810} {:answer/id #uuid "5a534cbd-b55c-5d09-9c91-89ab2b8eb3b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "242\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319811} {:answer/id #uuid "b90daaf2-193c-5207-9c2d-ec3219859c05" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "264\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319812} {:answer/id #uuid "1067141d-ea37-50a7-b39a-89ee67922d10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "280\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319813}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c904c404-150b-5876-98af-45d26b5ab085" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "253\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319809} :answer-field/id #uuid "3636667f-4a46-58be-bb02-32168fc619f5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319808}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f249579b-a99f-5b51-b0c7-e7f72d9a4512" :question/math-academy-id "q-163678" :question/problem "A particle $P$ of mass $2\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of a resultant force. The particle's acceleration is $(3{t}^{2}+4)\\,{m/s}^{2},$ where $t\\ge 0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v=1\\,m/s$ when $t=0\\,s,$ calculate the work done by the force between the times $t=1\\,s$ and $t=2\\,s.$" :question/worked-solution "The work-energy principle states that the work done by a resultant force on a particle equals the change in the particle's kinetic energy. That is,\n\n$$\nW=Δ\\,\\text{KE}\n$$\n\nwhere the change in kinetic energy of a particle is given by\n\n$$\nΔ\\,\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2}\n$$\n\nand $m$ is the particle's mass, ${v}_{0}$ is its initial velocity, and ${v}_{1}$ is its final velocity.\n\nThe acceleration of the particle is given by $a(t)=3{t}^{2}+4,$ where $t$ is time in seconds.\nWe find the velocity $v(t)$ of the particle by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (3{t}^{2}+4)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={t}^{3}+4t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(0)=1.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n1=(0)^{3}+4(0)+C\\,⟹\\,C=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)={t}^{3}+4t+1.$\nIn particular, the particle's initial velocity, at $t=1,$ is\n\n$$\n{v}_{0}=v(1)=(1)^{3}+4(1)+1=6\\,m/s,\n$$\n\nand final velocity, at $t=2,$ is\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=v(2)=(2)^{3}+4(2)+1=17\\,m/s.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the work-energy principle, the work done by the force between these times is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =Δ\\,\\text{KE} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot {17}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot {6}^{2} \\\\  & =289-36 \\\\  & =253\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319814 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "94904fa2-bf02-5486-950c-979a4d12998e" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Work-Energy Principle To Find the Work Done by a Force"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d339250c-ae5f-59d7-b0d6-356226875b11" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "160\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319816} {:answer/id #uuid "8398f076-a7e6-50b9-be2e-5d3e95fc050f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "144\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319817} {:answer/id #uuid "8908ab92-036a-502b-a519-5840d51f8d74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "96\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319818} {:answer/id #uuid "e1026fa0-8384-5b63-8da3-007754e8cb07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "125\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319819} {:answer/id #uuid "93e03990-aec5-50f0-b99c-a878c380f545" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "81\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319820}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8398f076-a7e6-50b9-be2e-5d3e95fc050f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "144\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319817} :answer-field/id #uuid "69e1319a-af25-5cd1-8f89-d2d33ce32195" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319815}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5a3311a4-5787-5c58-b32e-c36708f293d0" :question/math-academy-id "q-163679" :question/problem "A particle $P$ of mass $6\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of a resultant force. The particle's acceleration is $(\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}+1)\\,{m/s}^{2},$ where $t\\ge 0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v=2\\,m/s$ when $t=0\\,s,$ calculate the work done by the force between the times $t=1\\,s$ and $t=4\\,s.$" :question/worked-solution "The work-energy principle states that the work done by a resultant force on a particle equals the change in the particle's kinetic energy. That is,\n\n$$\nW=Δ\\,\\text{KE}\n$$\n\nwhere the change in kinetic energy of a particle is given by\n\n$$\nΔ\\,\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2}\n$$\n\nand $m$ is the particle's mass, ${v}_{0}$ is its initial velocity, and ${v}_{1}$ is its final velocity.\n\nThe acceleration of the particle is given by $a(t)=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}+1,$ where $t$ is time in seconds.\nWe find the velocity $v(t)$ of the particle by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}+1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{t}+t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(0)=2.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n2=\\sqrt{0}+0+C\\,⟹\\,C=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=\\sqrt{t}+t+2.$\nIn particular, the particle's initial velocity, at $t=1,$ is\n\n$$\n{v}_{0}=v(1)=\\sqrt{1}+1+2=4\\,m/s,\n$$\n\nand final velocity, at $t=4$, is\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=v(4)=\\sqrt{4}+4+2=8\\,m/s.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the work-energy principle, the work done by the force between these times is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =Δ\\,\\text{KE} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 6\\cdot {8}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 6\\cdot {4}^{2} \\\\  & =192-48 \\\\  & =144\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319821 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "94904fa2-bf02-5486-950c-979a4d12998e" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Work-Energy Principle To Find the Work Done by a Force"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04be146f-a72b-55b2-9d9f-1e383917016b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "72\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319823} {:answer/id #uuid "ea06e951-771d-5700-a6a1-1455ba04e05d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "51\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319824} {:answer/id #uuid "342ca4f4-2c50-5634-a29b-ba7d30784c55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "65\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319825} {:answer/id #uuid "48eb4e8d-b1e3-53d7-a29e-de507e4073b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "46\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319826} {:answer/id #uuid "58c2bad2-ee9f-5193-b8ba-5b27117089ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "58\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319827}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "342ca4f4-2c50-5634-a29b-ba7d30784c55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "65\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319825} :answer-field/id #uuid "8b9f44af-3302-5509-a07d-1f806704499a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319822}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1265cc6d-ceba-5ce8-a963-f281e796ac55" :question/math-academy-id "q-163680" :question/problem "A particle $P$ of mass $8\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of a resultant force. The particle's acceleration is $(2t-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}})\\,{m/s}^{2},$ where $t\\ge 1$ is the time in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v=2\\,m/s$ when $t=1\\,s,$ calculate the work done by the force between the times $t=1\\,s$ and $t=2\\,s.$" :question/worked-solution "The work-energy principle states that the work done by a resultant force on a particle equals the change in the particle's kinetic energy. That is,\n\n$$\nW=Δ\\,\\text{KE}\n$$\n\nwhere the change in kinetic energy of a particle is given by\n\n$$\nΔ\\,\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2}\n$$\n\nand $m$ is the particle's mass, ${v}_{0}$ is its initial velocity, and ${v}_{1}$ is its final velocity.\n\nThe acceleration of the particle is given by $a(t)=2t-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}},$ where $t$ is time in seconds.\nWe find the velocity $v(t)$ of the particle by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (2t-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={t}^{2}+\\frac{1}{t}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(1)=2.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n2={1}^{2}+\\frac{1}{1}+C\\,⟹\\,C=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)={t}^{2}+\\frac{1}{t}.$\nIn particular, the particle's initial velocity, at $t=1,$ is\n\n$$\n{v}_{0}=v(1)=2\\,m/s,\n$$\n\nand final velocity, at $t=2,$ is\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=v(2)={2}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{9}{2}\\,m/s.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the work-energy principle, the work done by the force between these times is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =Δ\\,\\text{KE} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 8\\cdot {(\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 8\\cdot {2}^{2} \\\\  & =81-16 \\\\  & =65\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319828 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9696b3f4-42c8-5796-be3a-6c59c9600c95" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Work Done by a Force"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "170caac7-537f-5c33-83b6-6c8d6663d3f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319830} {:answer/id #uuid "aa5e028f-a584-5d04-904f-333a80c1b31c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319831} {:answer/id #uuid "0c2508b5-7dff-50f1-8330-562e0e9ffa88" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319832} {:answer/id #uuid "dbc595fd-c05a-5a37-9246-b109b7f52df2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319833} {:answer/id #uuid "a61ac1a6-cf1c-5ec6-a812-c65f0b7570db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319834}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "170caac7-537f-5c33-83b6-6c8d6663d3f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319830} :answer-field/id #uuid "2446c211-e40c-519d-822e-3b3e250f33e7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319829}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d31a00a5-c404-50d8-b7f2-fda34cf2cc53" :question/math-academy-id "q-163685" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of a force of magnitude $\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{N}$ acting in the direction of increasing $x.$ Given that $x$ is measured in meters, calculate the work done by the force in moving the particle from $x=1$ to $x=4.$" :question/worked-solution "The work done by a force $F=F(x)$ in moving a particle from $x={x}_{1}$ to $x={x}_{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\nW={\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}F(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWhen the force and distance are measured in Newtons and meters, respectively, the work done by the force is measured in Joules ($J$).\n\nSubstituting the given values into our integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{x}\\,{|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =-(\\frac{1}{4}-1) \\\\  & =-(-\\frac{3}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319835 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9696b3f4-42c8-5796-be3a-6c59c9600c95" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Work Done by a Force"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b98165d9-b7fb-50e1-a888-eaebaea642fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{21}{2}\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319837} {:answer/id #uuid "0065a181-cfa5-5478-9178-21cc4a9afcd9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{55}{6}\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319838} {:answer/id #uuid "19d15234-0fb9-594f-a6f3-a3a88e27c15e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319839} {:answer/id #uuid "54875c30-d61b-518a-9d41-e77b69a350f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{2}\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319840} {:answer/id #uuid "86a85607-1b37-51f7-8bf1-b88bfb99ddad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319841}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0065a181-cfa5-5478-9178-21cc4a9afcd9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{55}{6}\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319838} :answer-field/id #uuid "195f8261-fa3a-5f1a-870b-078352af33ea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319836}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7a3795e2-15db-589d-bc2e-076ba2e315ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-163690" :question/problem "A particle $P$ of mass $2.5\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis with an acceleration of magnitude $(4-{x}^{2})\\,{m/s}^{2}$ acting in the direction $OP.$ Given that $x$ is measured in meters, calculate the work done by the force in moving the particle from $x=0$ to $x=1.$" :question/worked-solution "The work done by a force $F=F(x)$ in moving a particle from $x={x}_{1}$ to $x={x}_{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\nW={\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}F(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWhen the force and distance are measured in Newtons and meters, respectively, the work done by the force is measured in Joules ($J$).\n\nUsing Newton's second law of motion, the force required to accelerate a particle of mass $2.5\\,kg$ at a rate of $(4-{x}^{2})\\,{m/s}^{2}$ is\n\n$$\nF=ma=2.5\\cdot (4-{x}^{2})=(10-2.5{x}^{2})\\,N.\n$$\n\nNow, substituting the known values into our integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(10-2.5{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[10x-\\frac{5}{6}{x}^{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(10(1)-\\frac{5}{6}({1}^{3}))-(10(0)-\\frac{5}{6}({0}^{3})) \\\\  & =10-\\frac{5}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{55}{6}\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319842 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9696b3f4-42c8-5796-be3a-6c59c9600c95" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Work Done by a Force"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d61935a-1763-554a-9df1-087fba679411" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{e}^{4}\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319844} {:answer/id #uuid "cf5605c7-af67-5dba-af4f-75c72edb12e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({e}^{4}+3)\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319845} {:answer/id #uuid "2af63384-cdb9-5d5a-8e0b-39eaf952d327" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4({e}^{4}+1)\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319846} {:answer/id #uuid "694d2fa5-b6fb-56d8-8395-ebeb49cee6f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2{e}^{4}+3)\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319847} {:answer/id #uuid "45c05f59-793f-56c7-9345-37eebe567217" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({e}^{4}+1)\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319848}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cf5605c7-af67-5dba-af4f-75c72edb12e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({e}^{4}+3)\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319845} :answer-field/id #uuid "5bef13e4-7df2-5e1e-a261-d9bdb99bb8f0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319843}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d2ecb6c9-6f8f-54e1-be19-d2a5115c26c3" :question/math-academy-id "q-163693" :question/problem "A particle $P$ of mass $4\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis with an acceleration of magnitude $(\\frac{1}{x}+{x}^{3})\\,{m/s}^{2}$ acting in the direction $OP.$ Given that $x$ is measured in meters, calculate the work done by the force in moving the particle from $x=1$ to $x=e.$" :question/worked-solution "The work done by a force $F=F(x)$ in moving a particle from $x={x}_{1}$ to $x={x}_{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\nW={\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}F(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWhen the force and distance are measured in Newtons and meters, respectively, the work done by the force is measured in Joules ($J$).\n\nUsing Newton's second law of motion, the force required to accelerate a particle of mass $4\\,kg$ at a rate of $(\\frac{1}{x}+{x}^{3})\\,{m/s}^{2}$ is\n\n$$\nF=ma=4(\\frac{1}{x}+{x}^{3})=(\\frac{4}{x}+4{x}^{3})\\,N.\n$$\n\nNow, substituting the known values into our integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}(\\frac{4}{x}+4{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[4\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+{x}^{4}]}_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{ln}⁡e+{e}^{4}-(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡1+{1}^{4}) \\\\  & =4+{e}^{4}-0-1 \\\\  & =({e}^{4}+3)\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319849 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "793154ca-29f0-5552-97cd-57acbc915966" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Work-Energy Principle to Find a Particle's Velocity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "420d7de7-2314-53d6-a1b6-5b8dea09cfb7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319851} {:answer/id #uuid "85d0cc88-8c8b-56d2-9a0e-386c1496ef8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{10}\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319852} {:answer/id #uuid "b84d2e9e-c305-521e-a5bb-4d9b70eac1af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319853} {:answer/id #uuid "1ca1a8f0-d408-5e3c-96bc-3f23221b5ac1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{5}\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319854} {:answer/id #uuid "644243de-f3d8-50f1-812d-f35b3a8f984e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{10}\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319855}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "644243de-f3d8-50f1-812d-f35b3a8f984e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{10}\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319855} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a424332-2a32-5e46-afcc-11dd0bbbfac6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319850}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4f47de47-dd4b-5edd-a08d-f716e491cc12" :question/math-academy-id "q-163694" :question/problem "A particle $P$ of mass $6\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of a force of magnitude $(8-x)\\,N$ acting in the direction of increasing $x.$ Given that $x$ is measured in meters, and the particle has velocity $v=6\\,m/s$ at $x=1,$ calculate the velocity of the particle at $x=3.$" :question/worked-solution "The work-energy principle states that the work done by a resultant force on a particle equals the change in the particle's kinetic energy. That is,\n\n$$\nW=Δ\\,\\text{KE}\n$$\n\nwhere the change in kinetic energy of a particle is given by\n\n$$\nΔ\\,\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2}\n$$\n\nand $m$ is the particle's mass, ${v}_{0}$ is its initial velocity, and ${v}_{1}$ is its final velocity.\n\nFirst, we calculate the work done by the force in moving the particle from $x=1$ to $x=3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & ={\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}F(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}(8-x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[8x-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =(24-\\frac{9}{2})-(8-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =12\\,J\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe particle has velocity $v=6\\,m/s$ at $x=1.$ So, we can find the velocity ${v}_{1}$ at $x=3$ using the work-energy principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2} \\\\ 12 & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 6\\cdot {v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 6\\cdot {6}^{2} \\\\ 12 & =3{v}_{1}^{2}-108 \\\\ 3{v}_{1} & =120 \\\\ {v}_{1}^{2} & =40 \\\\ {v}_{1} & =\\pm \\sqrt{40} \\\\ {v}_{1} & =\\pm 2\\sqrt{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can disregard the negative solution since the velocity and acceleration of the particle are positive in the regions under consideration. \n\nTherefore, the velocity of the particle at $x=3$ is $2\\sqrt{10}\\,m/s.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319856 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9696b3f4-42c8-5796-be3a-6c59c9600c95" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Work Done by a Force"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ad73553f-0ddd-516a-8693-f0126079469c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "28\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319858} {:answer/id #uuid "dba6ee6b-2cfa-563d-9e1b-abb31ceb6e07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319859} {:answer/id #uuid "c2e4cc83-4bd5-5d72-bc4e-389288f52437" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319860} {:answer/id #uuid "3158797f-8dcc-5502-82f3-5da592570408" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319861} {:answer/id #uuid "648a98a7-0f6e-56b9-894f-745354b4e10c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319862}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ad73553f-0ddd-516a-8693-f0126079469c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "28\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319858} :answer-field/id #uuid "25e394c9-d4f2-56d3-befc-093e847c1fa3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319857}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d8e7d1ad-f8e5-5653-a444-d370087e763d" :question/math-academy-id "q-163695" :question/problem "A particle $P$ of mass $3.5\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis with an acceleration of magnitude $2{x}^{3}\\,{m/s}^{2}$ acting in the direction $OP.$ Given that $x$ is measured in meters, calculate the work done by the force in moving the particle from $x=0$ to $x=2.$" :question/worked-solution "The work done by a force $F=F(x)$ in moving a particle from $x={x}_{1}$ to $x={x}_{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\nW={\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}F(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWhen the force and distance are measured in Newtons and meters, respectively, the work done by the force is measured in Joules ($J$).\n\nUsing Newton's second law of motion, the force required to accelerate a particle of mass $3.5\\,kg$ at a rate of $2{x}^{3}\\,{m/s}^{2}$ is\n\n$$\nF=ma=3.5\\cdot 2{x}^{3}=7{x}^{3}\\,N.\n$$\n\nNow, substituting the known values into our integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}7{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{4}{x}^{4}\\,{|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{4}(16-0) \\\\  & =28\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319863 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "793154ca-29f0-5552-97cd-57acbc915966" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Work-Energy Principle to Find a Particle's Velocity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e7a3a42-c132-5d2a-aa26-3306c8a7ccdb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{3}\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319865} {:answer/id #uuid "ae588ca3-deff-5082-9d56-92745139b71c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319866} {:answer/id #uuid "8e82da4f-02b1-50ae-8dad-b0e02d05102e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{15}\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319867} {:answer/id #uuid "5da9d34e-ead9-539c-beb4-43005ca82413" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{5}\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319868} {:answer/id #uuid "ab2156ad-e394-55cd-a2ba-9102817ce7aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{15}\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319869}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ae588ca3-deff-5082-9d56-92745139b71c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319866} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a3224af-78e6-535d-86ba-a6a8293e3386" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319864}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d376408f-8c1d-565f-93b3-1f70d2fa1b5e" :question/math-academy-id "q-163696" :question/problem "A particle $P$ of mass $3\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of the force of magnitude $(\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}+x)\\,N$ acting in the direction of increasing $x.$ Given that $x$ is measured in meters, the particle was initially at $x=1,$ and the particle has velocity $v=3\\,m/s$ at $x=4,$ calculate the particle's initial velocity." :question/worked-solution "The work-energy principle states that the work done by a resultant force on a particle equals the change in the particle's kinetic energy. That is,\n\n$$\nW=Δ\\,\\text{KE}\n$$\n\nwhere the change in kinetic energy of a particle is given by\n\n$$\nΔ\\,\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2}\n$$\n\nand $m$ is the particle's mass, ${v}_{0}$ is its initial velocity, and ${v}_{1}$ is its final velocity.\n\nFirst, we calculate the work done by the force in moving the particle from $x=1$ to $x=4:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & ={\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}F(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}(\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}+x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{2}{x}){|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =(8-\\frac{1}{2})-(\\frac{1}{2}-2) \\\\  & =9\\,J\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe particle has velocity $v=3\\,m/s$ at $x=4.$ So, we can find the velocity ${v}_{0}$ at $x=1$ using the work-energy principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =\\frac{1}{2}{m}_{1}{v}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2} \\\\ 9 & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot {3}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot {v}_{0}^{2} \\\\ 9 & =\\frac{27}{2}-\\frac{3}{2}{v}_{0}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}{v}_{0}^{2} & =\\frac{9}{2} \\\\ {v}_{0}^{2} & =3 \\\\ {v}_{0} & =\\pm \\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can disregard the negative solution since the velocity and acceleration of the particle are positive in the regions under consideration. \n\nTherefore, the velocity of the particle at $x=1$ is $\\sqrt{3}\\,m/s.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319870 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "793154ca-29f0-5552-97cd-57acbc915966" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Work-Energy Principle to Find a Particle's Velocity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2800b363-8033-54e7-aad0-ab700bad0eac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319872} {:answer/id #uuid "ce47bd81-f9fc-5448-a9d2-96e00e989e89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319873} {:answer/id #uuid "8a7b4e96-e2c4-535a-8bda-3d906a3a02d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{7}\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319874} {:answer/id #uuid "8b3ad5ea-871b-5e99-87b1-dece7d2ff0a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319875} {:answer/id #uuid "0b5fbd66-ce0f-5790-8183-b9b8bc751a61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{7}\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319876}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8b3ad5ea-871b-5e99-87b1-dece7d2ff0a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319875} :answer-field/id #uuid "e5845577-161c-57a8-be35-c0c1851f5d6c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319871}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2fcbb421-9d54-5162-95bd-edc60b0635c5" :question/math-academy-id "q-163698" :question/problem "A particle $P$ of mass $7\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of the force of magnitude $(2x+3)\\,N$ acting in the direction of increasing $x.$ Given that $x$ is measured in meters, the particle was initially at $x=1,$ and the particle has velocity $v=2\\sqrt{5}\\,m/s$ at $x=3,$ calculate the particle's initial velocity." :question/worked-solution "The work-energy principle states that the work done by a resultant force on a particle equals the change in the particle's kinetic energy. That is,\n\n$$\nW=Δ\\,\\text{KE}\n$$\n\nwhere the change in kinetic energy of a particle is given by\n\n$$\nΔ\\,\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2}\n$$\n\nand $m$ is the particle's mass, ${v}_{0}$ is its initial velocity, and ${v}_{1}$ is its final velocity.\n\nFirst, we calculate the work done by the force in moving the particle from $x=1$ to $x=3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & ={\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}F(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}(2x+3)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+3x){|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =(9+9)-(1+3) \\\\  & =14\\,J\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe particle has velocity $v=2\\sqrt{5}\\,m/s$ at $x=3.$ So, we can find the velocity ${v}_{0}$ at $x=1$ using the work-energy principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =\\frac{1}{2}{m}_{1}{v}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2} \\\\ 14 & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 7\\cdot (2\\sqrt{5})^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 7\\cdot {v}_{0}^{2} \\\\ 14 & =70-\\frac{7}{2}{v}_{0}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{7}{2}{v}_{0}^{2} & =56 \\\\ {v}_{0}^{2} & =16 \\\\ {v}_{0} & =\\pm 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can disregard the negative solution since the velocity and acceleration of the particle are positive in the regions under consideration. \n\nTherefore, the velocity of the particle at $x=1$ is $4\\,m/s.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319877 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "793154ca-29f0-5552-97cd-57acbc915966" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Work-Energy Principle to Find a Particle's Velocity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dfe6515d-c0e6-5415-b950-5591730d9c27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319879} {:answer/id #uuid "0fa0480e-9467-524b-a695-9a91915c667a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319880} {:answer/id #uuid "5c2073a7-03f1-5a41-8e2e-9d250005a8aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319881} {:answer/id #uuid "2dc6c981-1087-521c-a58b-28a877111421" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319882} {:answer/id #uuid "451e1661-d538-504d-b439-536fe18759ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319883}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5c2073a7-03f1-5a41-8e2e-9d250005a8aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319881} :answer-field/id #uuid "c702e0be-4a89-5e91-b790-5bf6d7d350f4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319878}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "78314a43-9958-56d6-8eec-89735ec4ff15" :question/math-academy-id "q-163700" :question/problem "A particle $P$ of mass $4\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of the force of magnitude $({x}^{3}-4)\\,N$ acting in the direction of increasing $x.$ Given that $x$ is measured in meters, the particle was initially at $x=3$, and the particle has velocity $v=10\\,m/s$ at $x=5,$ calculate the particle's initial velocity." :question/worked-solution "The work-energy principle states that the work done by a resultant force on a particle equals the change in the particle's kinetic energy. That is,\n\n$$\nW=Δ\\,\\text{KE}\n$$\n\nwhere the change in kinetic energy of a particle is given by\n\n$$\nΔ\\,\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2}\n$$\n\nand $m$ is the particle's mass, ${v}_{0}$ is its initial velocity, and ${v}_{1}$ is its final velocity.\n\nFirst, we calculate the work done by the force in moving the particle from $x=3$ to $x=5:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & ={\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}F(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3}^{5}({x}^{3}-4)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}-4x){|}_{3}^{5} \\\\  & =(\\frac{625}{4}-20)-(\\frac{81}{4}-12) \\\\  & =128\\,J\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe particle has velocity $v=10\\,m/s$ at $x=5.$ So, we can find the velocity ${v}_{0}$ at $x=3$ using the work-energy principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =\\frac{1}{2}{m}_{1}{v}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2} \\\\ 128 & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4\\cdot {10}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4\\cdot {v}_{0}^{2} \\\\ 128 & =200-2{v}_{0}^{2} \\\\ 2{v}_{0}^{2} & =72 \\\\ {v}_{0}^{2} & =36 \\\\ {v}_{0} & =\\pm 6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can disregard the negative solution since the velocity and acceleration of the particle are positive in the regions under consideration. \n\nTherefore, the velocity of the particle at $x=3$ is $6\\,m/s.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319884 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "793154ca-29f0-5552-97cd-57acbc915966" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Work-Energy Principle to Find a Particle's Velocity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a0a9d7d4-87d2-523c-86b2-bb1bb56204eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319886} {:answer/id #uuid "c17366a5-2ffc-5309-bee6-f999ba8ea6ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319887} {:answer/id #uuid "b7430fbf-3739-53db-a6d7-de1ff077c075" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{4}\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319888} {:answer/id #uuid "acd35926-569e-5eef-a520-1277dae86940" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319889} {:answer/id #uuid "6d41d700-3933-5db9-9b5b-427d8a235c52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319890}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a0a9d7d4-87d2-523c-86b2-bb1bb56204eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319886} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc0b93d0-7729-5089-b09b-93db67a03ee9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319885}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "89e50146-b1c6-571f-ad82-cc9a4f7f02cf" :question/math-academy-id "q-163702" :question/problem "A particle $P$ of mass $1\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of the force of magnitude $(3{x}^{2}+2)\\,N$ acting in the direction of increasing $x.$ Given that distances along the $x$-axis are measured in meters, the particle is initially at the origin, and the particle has velocity $v=7\\,m/s$ at $x=2,$ find the particle's change in velocity in moving from $x=0$ to $x=2.$" :question/worked-solution "The work-energy principle states that the work done by a resultant force on a particle equals the change in the particle's kinetic energy. That is,\n\n$$\nW=Δ\\,\\text{KE}\n$$\n\nwhere the change in kinetic energy of a particle is given by\n\n$$\nΔ\\,\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2}\n$$\n\nand $m$ is the particle's mass, ${v}_{0}$ is its initial velocity, and ${v}_{1}$ is its final velocity.\n\nFirst, we calculate the work done by the force in moving the particle from $x=0$ to $x=2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & ={\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}F(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(3{x}^{2}+2)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[{x}^{3}+2x]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =8+4-0 \\\\  & =12\\,J\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe particle has velocity $v=7\\,m/s$ at $x=2.$ So, we can find the velocity ${v}_{0}$ at $x=0$ using the work-energy principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2} \\\\ 12 & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1\\cdot {7}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1\\cdot {v}_{0}^{2} \\\\ 12 & =\\frac{49}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}{v}_{0}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{v}_{0}^{2} & =\\frac{25}{2} \\\\ {v}_{0}^{2} & =25 \\\\ {v}_{0} & =\\pm 5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can disregard the negative solution since the velocity and acceleration of the particle are positive in the regions under consideration. \n\nTherefore, the velocity of the particle at $x=0$ is $5\\,m/s.$\n\nFinally, the particle's change in velocity from $x=0$ to $x=2$ is\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}-{v}_{0}=7-5=2\\,m/s.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319891 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ec390427-7510-57ea-aedf-a930284f93b5" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Kinetic Energy of a Particle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a62c5499-ceb3-5425-8fb0-a64eed0c7418" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319893} {:answer/id #uuid "9e2efc61-69ca-56b8-b6ae-7b684774f0c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319894} {:answer/id #uuid "1bbdd9a2-dd5b-5fac-9d5b-e64eb2b1dfc3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319895} {:answer/id #uuid "624c90e1-c945-5860-acbf-26c240ce44cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319896} {:answer/id #uuid "c34bdcf0-eb2e-5eb9-ae12-ac65158b6573" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319897}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "624c90e1-c945-5860-acbf-26c240ce44cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319896} :answer-field/id #uuid "ecd01acf-c43e-59a5-b631-564778c16205" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319892}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d94c161c-03c9-560a-9c5f-c8ce19e4cb0a" :question/math-academy-id "q-163705" :question/problem "A particle $P$ of mass $4\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis from rest at the origin with an acceleration of magnitude ${t}^{3}\\,{m/s}^{2},$ where the acceleration acts in the direction of increasing $x,$ and $t\\ge 0$ is the time in seconds. Calculate the particle's kinetic energy at time $t=2\\,s.$" :question/worked-solution "The kinetic energy of a particle with mass $m$ and velocity $v$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWhen the mass and velocity are measured in kilograms and meters per second, respectively, the particle's kinetic energy is measured in Joules ($J$).\n\nFirst, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ v(t) & =\\int {t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ v(t) & =\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{4}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(0)=0.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n0=\\frac{1}{4}(0)^{4}+C\\,⟹\\,C=0.\n$$\n\nHence, the velocity of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{4}.$ In particular, its velocity at time $t=2\\,s$ is\n\n$$\nv=v(2)=\\frac{1}{4}({2}^{4})=4\\,m/s.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the kinetic energy of the particle at $t=2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{KE} & =\\frac{1}{2}m{v}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4\\cdot {4}^{2} \\\\  & =32\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319898 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ec390427-7510-57ea-aedf-a930284f93b5" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Kinetic Energy of a Particle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "927d20c5-f235-5d50-b2a3-d911d7d19bd2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3}\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319900} {:answer/id #uuid "2ae6bf1e-455a-5914-aa0e-7c5671ec5aa4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{2}\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319901} {:answer/id #uuid "073465f6-0189-5f33-afd6-8c8044c68402" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{2}\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319902} {:answer/id #uuid "71ca9f64-c959-5ce4-a26b-923da4a4aaee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{3}\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319903} {:answer/id #uuid "e5f4ca8e-9810-5671-b0df-3199a5c2b379" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{6}\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319904}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "71ca9f64-c959-5ce4-a26b-923da4a4aaee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{3}\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319903} :answer-field/id #uuid "7002ed18-f578-5c2c-83f1-99cad28e62f7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319899}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f7b3785d-8d10-54ef-aace-746016f1c83f" :question/math-academy-id "q-163708" :question/problem "A particle $P$ of mass $6\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis from rest at the origin with an acceleration of magnitude $(1+{t}^{2})\\,{m/s}^{2},$ where the acceleration acts in the direction of increasing $x,$ and $t\\ge 0$ is the time in seconds. Calculate the particle's kinetic energy at time $t=1\\,s.$" :question/worked-solution "The kinetic energy of a particle with mass $m$ and velocity $v$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWhen the mass and velocity are measured in kilograms and meters per second, respectively, the particle's kinetic energy is measured in Joules ($J$).\n\nFirst, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ v(t) & =\\int (1+{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ v(t) & =t+\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{3}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(0)=0.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n0=0+\\frac{1}{3}({0}^{3})+C\\,⟹\\,C=0.\n$$\n\nHence, the velocity of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=t+\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{3}.$ In particular, its velocity at time $t=1\\,s$ is\n\n$$\nv=v(1)=1+\\frac{1}{3}({1}^{3})=\\frac{4}{3}\\,m/s.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the kinetic energy of the particle at $t=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{KE} & =\\frac{1}{2}m{v}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 6\\cdot {(\\frac{4}{3})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 6\\cdot \\frac{16}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{3}\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319905 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ec390427-7510-57ea-aedf-a930284f93b5" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Kinetic Energy of a Particle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "77bd545f-33ca-554b-ae98-ede488c8febe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319907} {:answer/id #uuid "38092463-c428-5b54-a9c9-f2312f2aa7ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{6}\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319908} {:answer/id #uuid "3842fc37-8450-5bae-8c18-ce70a71d6c45" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{21}{8}\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319909} {:answer/id #uuid "8055d7d2-8273-5a6f-b1d7-0a6554e47d70" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{4}\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319910} {:answer/id #uuid "d8da6c80-68c3-5137-887c-bee64da01315" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319911}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3842fc37-8450-5bae-8c18-ce70a71d6c45" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{21}{8}\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319909} :answer-field/id #uuid "dad38db8-663f-5e65-9ab8-975723b08fcf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319906}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f633b7bf-41b1-55e5-94fa-734d0d539fd4" :question/math-academy-id "q-163711" :question/problem "A particle $P$ of mass $3\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis from rest at the origin with an acceleration of magnitude $\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}\\,{m/s}^{2},$ where the acceleration acts in the direction of increasing $x,$ and $t\\ge 0$ is the time in seconds. Calculate the change in kinetic energy of the particle between the times $t=1\\,s$ and $t=2\\,s.$" :question/worked-solution "The change in kinetic energy of a particle with mass $m,$ initial velocity ${v}_{0},$ and final velocity ${v}_{1}$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔ\\,\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWhen the mass and velocity are measured in kilograms and meters per second, respectively, the particle's kinetic energy is measured in Joules ($J$).\n\nFirst, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ v(t) & =\\int \\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ v(t) & =\\frac{1}{6}{t}^{3}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(0)=0.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n0=\\frac{1}{6}({0}^{3})+C\\,⟹\\,C=0.\n$$\n\nHence, the velocity of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=\\frac{1}{6}{t}^{3}.$ In particular, its inital velocity at time $t=1\\,s$ and final velocity at time $t=2\\,s$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{0} & =v(1)=\\frac{1}{6}({1}^{3})=\\frac{1}{6}\\,m/s, \\\\ {v}_{1} & =v(2)=\\frac{1}{6}({2}^{3})=\\frac{4}{3}\\,m/s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the change in kinetic energy of the particle between these times is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ\\,\\text{KE} & =\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot {(\\frac{4}{3})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{6})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}-\\frac{1}{24} \\\\  & =\\frac{21}{8}\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319912 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ec390427-7510-57ea-aedf-a930284f93b5" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Kinetic Energy of a Particle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6189bdf9-d70c-55ca-a0f4-08991a938b25" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319914} {:answer/id #uuid "c1c031c5-537f-5404-9260-c1160ad7b8b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319915} {:answer/id #uuid "939c7de0-affc-5ac8-87b5-70f68e10e9b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319916} {:answer/id #uuid "d8a45f19-5086-5d4a-8872-3531ba24be14" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319917} {:answer/id #uuid "6985645b-3394-51a6-87a9-3b9b35fed3ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319918}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "939c7de0-affc-5ac8-87b5-70f68e10e9b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319916} :answer-field/id #uuid "a6251978-0f83-5ddf-9b77-7594f945ef7e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319913}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ce04dae5-4009-5660-88a7-083f025f0df3" :question/math-academy-id "q-163712" :question/problem "A particle $P$ of mass $2\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis from rest at the origin with an acceleration of magnitude $2{e}^{-t}\\,{m/s}^{2},$ where the acceleration acts in the direction of increasing $x,$ and $t\\ge 0$ is the time in seconds. Calculate the change in kinetic energy of the particle between the times $t=\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\,s$ and $t=\\operatorname{ln}⁡4\\,s.$" :question/worked-solution "The change in kinetic energy of a particle with mass $m,$ initial velocity ${v}_{0},$ and final velocity ${v}_{1}$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔ\\,\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWhen the mass and velocity are measured in kilograms and meters per second, respectively, the particle's kinetic energy is measured in Joules ($J$).\n\nFirst, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ v(t) & =\\int 2{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ v(t) & =-2{e}^{-t}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(0)=0.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n0=-2{e}^{-0}+C\\,⟹\\,C=2.\n$$\n\nHence, the velocity of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=2-2{e}^{-t}.$ In particular, its inital velocity at time $t=\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\,s$ and final velocity at time $t=\\operatorname{ln}⁡4\\,s$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{0} & =v(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)=2-2{e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}=1\\,m/s, \\\\ {v}_{1} & =v(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4)=2-2{e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}=\\frac{3}{2}\\,m/s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the change in kinetic energy of the particle between these times is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ\\,\\text{KE} & =\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot {(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot {(1)}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}-1 \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319919 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ec390427-7510-57ea-aedf-a930284f93b5" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Kinetic Energy of a Particle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f540567-b661-5bca-8f06-ce7188bb3bef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319921} {:answer/id #uuid "b3f00337-993c-58a0-939a-72a28200cffe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319922} {:answer/id #uuid "f1ad6484-6f17-5a7c-99e6-71ee26604b37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319923} {:answer/id #uuid "6235e8f9-7324-5bf9-81ad-1f3ed9e5d84b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319924} {:answer/id #uuid "d67c3d5a-ca86-5f1f-95d7-2d63545f6854" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319925}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d67c3d5a-ca86-5f1f-95d7-2d63545f6854" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319925} :answer-field/id #uuid "76f1e80b-03d7-53d7-abe3-1ba9f0dda61a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319920}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8c495a30-23c5-530b-9d91-e188ad9ee72e" :question/math-academy-id "q-163714" :question/problem "A particle $P$ of mass $\\frac{8}{5}\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis from rest at the origin with an acceleration of magnitude $3t\\,{m/s}^{2},$ where the acceleration acts in the direction of increasing $x,$ and $t\\ge 0$ is the time in seconds. Calculate the change in kinetic energy of the particle between the times $t=1\\,s$ and $t=2\\,s.$" :question/worked-solution "The change in kinetic energy of a particle with mass $m,$ initial velocity ${v}_{0},$ and final velocity ${v}_{1}$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔ\\,\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWhen the mass and velocity are measured in kilograms and meters per second, respectively, the particle's kinetic energy is measured in Joules ($J$).\n\nFirst, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ v(t) & =\\int 3t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ v(t) & =\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(0)=0.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n0=\\frac{3}{2}({0}^{2})+C\\,⟹\\,C=0.\n$$\n\nHence, the velocity of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2}.$ In particular, its inital velocity at time $t=1\\,s$ and final velocity at time $t=2\\,s$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{0} & =v(1)=\\frac{3}{2}({1}^{2})=\\frac{3}{2}\\,m/s, \\\\ {v}_{1} & =v(2)=\\frac{3}{2}({2}^{2})=6\\,m/s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the change in kinetic energy of the particle between these times is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ\\,\\text{KE} & =\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{8}{5}\\cdot {6}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{8}{5}\\cdot {(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{144}{5}-\\frac{9}{5} \\\\  & =27\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
